Z||000007||DRH||03-09-2000||04:47 PM||||gdogg, welcome.<P>My stepson's friend said the police are always following him when he drives his girlfriend's 86 SE. Even doing u-turns to follow him for a couple of miles.<P>I don't remember the police ever going out of their way to follow me in my 88 Formula in the year I've had it. I don't drive particularly slow either... usually 5-10 over posted.<P>Both cars are black coupes with a wing. A non-Fiero person would be hard pressed to tell them apart at a glance.<P>The main difference I can see is that I'm 40 and he's 19... Not really fair, but often true...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||superXmotorsports||03-09-2000||10:12 PM||||Only been pulled over once in a Fiero (the red GT-R) the officer said "Wow, that's some car" and let me go.  Now the Spyder and BMW are another story... SXM.||||reg||12
Z||000009||turbo86se||03-09-2000||11:08 PM||||I got pulled over a year or so ago, and the police officer spent more time walking around my car than he did talking to me. It was obvious he wasn't looking for anything - just checking out my Fiero! No ticket, just "nice car and slow down!"  ||||reg||10
Z||000010||Phaeton||03-09-2000||11:43 PM||||Some cops are pretty nice, had one give me a ticket for 19 over the limit so I wouldn't have to go to court, his parting words, "that's a nice car, don't wreck it".<P>I was doing 40 over but he and I were the only cars on the road.  My focus was the pavement in front of me.||||reg||12
Z||000011||batboy||03-10-2000||10:42 AM||||Several years ago, a week after I bought my first Fiero (87 SE 2.5), I was driving back home from visiting my parents through Missouri at about midnight, 5 mph below the speed limit (I'd seen some deer run across the road earlier), when a young gung-ho county deputy pulled me over. He claimed I crossed the fog line (the painted line next to the shoulder) which of course I didn't, that was his excuse for pulling me over. I handed him me D.L. and when asked if I'd been drinking I told him no (just Mountain Dew to stay awake, honest). I was told to step out of the car, which I thought was highly unusual. As I got out, he grabbed me, threw me against the car and frisked me (WTF?), then sat me in the back seat of the cruiser. He asked what I was doing on this road so late at night, I told him. Then he said, pretty fancy car you have there. I replied that it was a 10 year old Pontiac that I had just bought for $2,000 recently. He looked disappointed. He asked if I had any weapons or contraband in the car and I said no. He said, then you won't mind if I search your car, right? Now, I worked in University Police back in college and was in the military police before that, so I know he was out of line. I told him NO, he was not searching my car because I had done nothing wrong and he had no probable cause for anything. He told me that he'd call the state police and have them bring out the drug dogs then. So that's it! He thought I was a big time drug smuggler or something. I told him if he wanted to waste time in calling out the dogs, then I had time to kill too, but he was NOT searching my car. I then told him I was considering filing a grievance against him for harassment. The dispatcher radioed the officer to tell him that I had no outstanding warrants for my arrest and that the car was not stolen. There was a long pause while we glared at each other, then he finally said, I'm going to let you off this time. <B>DUH!</B> There are good cops and there are bad cops.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Patrick's Dad||03-10-2000||10:07 PM||||I guess that there are good cops, and there are bad cops, and there are funny cops....<P>Before I had my Formula, I owned an '85 2M4, which was white with a large red stripe running mid hood up to the roof (It didn't continue to the decklid), and a red pattern from the front wheelwells that gathered to a small stripe along the side. Not that that really has anything to do with it, but I used to call it "The Anti-Theft Fiero," because it's the only one I've seen....<P>Anyway, I was driving home from my girlfriend's, and I was getting onto the Southeast Expressway (Rt 93S in Boston) from the Mass Pike (Rt 90E). It was 2 in the morning, and no one was on the road, so I opened up a bit. It wasn't even a mile when I was pulled over by a Boston Police Officer. He walked along my car and asked, "Do you know how fast you were going? No, of course you don't. Your spedometer only goes up to 85."||||reg||10
